The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
ret = plat_dat->init(pdev, plat_dat->bsp_priv);
if (ret)
    return ret;
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because if the `init()` function is called and it returns an error, the probe function exits without calling the `exit()` callback to clean up resources. This can lead to resource leaks or inconsistent states since any initialization done prior to the error is not undone.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
ret = stmmac_dvr_probe(&pdev->dev, plat_dat, &stmmac_res);
if (ret)
    goto err_exit;
...
if (plat_dat->exit)
    plat_dat->exit(pdev, plat_dat->bsp_priv);
```
Reason:
The fix introduces error handling after the call to `stmmac_dvr_probe()`. If an error occurs, it jumps to the `err_exit` label, where it checks if the `exit()` callback is defined and calls it to properly clean up resources, ensuring that any initialization effects are undone and preventing resource leaks.

